{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789491290,"nonce":"5AloO6H5AUcCcZgpLBRU5/gWBdU=","message":"The last two items, 2026-09-15:\n\n- A cell or a published class outliving its VM: landed in 100963e. At\n  heap_drop the Wren adapter severs every cell kept as a shadow on one\n  of the record's objects (cell::sever: object cleared, map entry gone,\n  trace marks nothing) and withdraws the modules it published\n  (registry::withdraw, sources included). A severed cell unwraps to the\n  gone object, a static core object whose protocol raises \"the object\n  is gone: the runtime that owned it was dropped\" on every send and\n  answers `gone object` as its type, so it stays that across further\n  crossings. An adopted instance in front of a Haxe object's cell leaves\n  it. Test: crates/caribou-interop/tests/gone.rs (Haxe's static keeps a\n  Wren function; the VM drops; Player.hit raises with the message; the\n  registry no longer has the class; a collection runs clean; a new VM\n  serves the same program). Without the sever the call reached the\n  dead VM and answered \"no Wren VM is entered\" — an error by luck of\n  vm_of's record check, not by design.\n- The raw-address residue the core's frames leave for the Wren cycle's\n  conservative scan: inherent to conservative scanning and harmless. A\n  stale word retains an object for one more cycle; it never dangles,\n  since retention is the safe direction. wrenref.rs keeps its scrub so\n  its \"went with the cycle\" assertion is deterministic. No change.\n\nClosing: proxies (cells), rooting, identity in both directions, and the\nlifetime past the VM are all in.","files":null}]}